#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca1a126-0de5-5dd4-9dfc-dc91ccd2ea49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309084} {:answer/id #uuid "7faf511e-bf00-51de-a619-877bd56f6290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309085} {:answer/id #uuid "35d7cc02-ae0f-588e-8ff0-cff145761007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309086} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed49497-1a24-5ebc-a9cd-d73701101f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309087} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b64c66-f74d-5d76-bfae-b5f783d4cc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed49497-1a24-5ebc-a9cd-d73701101f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309087} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7b849a-ca96-5579-b91e-f85a7aac7cd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "272ae442-0eb1-5b16-aba6-999ab95c4bf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-21504" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=3$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-12x+36,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+36 & =0 \\\\ -12(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=3.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(3)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=3,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=3\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44aa782b-d055-5d93-a386-a8b49b828205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309091} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4a7a56-3a5c-58a5-be69-0682d73bde4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309092} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cff171-ef3f-5d60-a9f7-a8307991cad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309093} {:answer/id #uuid "be13dfee-3409-5074-a64d-603766a3f42c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309094} {:answer/id #uuid "86953212-600f-5b8d-ad71-5809e02af13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44aa782b-d055-5d93-a386-a8b49b828205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309091} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec32751-2902-5efd-b882-aad408b5f4c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11a248cf-66d8-5ef2-be89-74a7b6dbe4ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-21814" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}-20x+30.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=4{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}-20x+30,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=20({x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-x-1).\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20(4{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =20(4(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =20(-4+3-1) \\\\  & =-40<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
